---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import SalaryCalculator from '@components/islands/SalaryCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'salary-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How do I convert an hourly wage to an annual salary?', answer: 'Multiply the hourly rate by the hours you work per week, then by 52 weeks. At $50 an hour over 40 hours that is $104,000 a year before any time off is taken into account. This calculator does it in every direction, and shows the figure again with holidays and vacation days accounted for.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between the unadjusted and adjusted columns?', answer: 'The unadjusted column ignores time off: it spreads your rate across all 260 weekdays of the year. The adjusted column subtracts your holidays and vacation days first, so it reflects the days you are actually paid for. At $50 an hour with 10 holidays and 15 vacation days, the year is $104,000 unadjusted and $94,000 adjusted.' },
  { question: 'Which column applies to me?', answer: 'If you are paid by the hour or the day, the adjusted column is closer to reality, because a day not worked is a day not paid. If you are on a fixed salary, the salary you were quoted is already the adjusted figure — your pay does not drop in a week containing a public holiday.' },
  { question: 'Why does entering an annual salary change the hourly rate?', answer: 'Because a quoted salary already covers your time off. To find the rate behind it the calculator divides by the days you actually work, not by every weekday — so a $94,000 salary with 25 days off works out at the same $50 an hour that produced it.' },
  { question: 'How many work hours are in a year?', answer: 'The common full-time assumption is 2,080 hours: 40 hours a week across 52 weeks, or 260 weekdays of 8 hours. Change hours per week and days per week to match your own schedule — a 37.5-hour week lands on a very different hourly figure.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between bi-weekly and semi-monthly pay?', answer: 'Bi-weekly means every two weeks, which is 26 paychecks a year. Semi-monthly means twice a month, which is only 24. The semi-monthly check is therefore the larger of the two for the same salary, which is why both rows appear separately.' },
  { question: 'Is this gross or net pay?', answer: 'Gross — before income tax, payroll taxes, retirement contributions or benefits are deducted. To estimate what you actually keep, use the income tax calculator.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} presentation="task-first" rail intro="Enter your pay at any one frequency and see every other, both ignoring time off and with your holidays and vacation days accounted for." faqs={faqs} reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-10' }}>
  <SalaryCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter a <strong>salary amount</strong> — any single figure you already know.</li>
      <li>Choose the <strong>period</strong> it is quoted in: per hour, day, week, bi-week, semi-month, month, quarter or year.</li>
      <li>Set your working year: <strong>hours per week</strong>, <strong>days per week</strong>,
        <strong>holidays per year</strong> and <strong>vacation days per year</strong>. The defaults
        are 40, 5, 10 and 15.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong> to fill in all eight frequencies, twice over.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The two columns, and why they differ</h2>
    <p>
      The same job can be described by two different annual numbers, and the difference between
      them is time off. The <strong>unadjusted</strong> column spreads your pay across every
      weekday in the year. The <strong>adjusted</strong> column takes your holidays and vacation
      days out first, leaving only the days you are actually paid for.
    </p>
    <p>
      Which one matters depends on how you are paid. Paid by the hour, a day not worked is a day
      not earned, so the adjusted column is the honest one. On a fixed salary, your pay does not
      shrink in a week containing a public holiday — the salary you were quoted <em>is</em> the
      adjusted figure, and the unadjusted column tells you what that salary is worth per day of
      work.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is also why the calculator reads your entry differently depending on the period you
      choose. An <strong>hourly or daily</strong> amount is pay for time worked, so it is taken as
      unadjusted. Any <strong>longer period</strong> is a salary that already covers your time off,
      so it is taken as adjusted.
    </p>

    <h2>The arithmetic</h2>
    <p>
      Everything is built on 52 weeks a year. Your days per week set the length of the working
      year, and your time off sets how much of it you are paid for:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Weekdays in a year</strong> = 52 × days per week. On a five-day week, 260.</li>
      <li><strong>Days actually worked</strong> = weekdays − holidays − vacation days. With 10 and 15, that is 235.</li>
      <li><strong>Unadjusted annual</strong> = daily rate × 260. <strong>Adjusted annual</strong> = daily rate × 235.</li>
      <li>Every other row divides its own column's annual figure: ÷ 4 for quarterly, ÷ 12 monthly,
        ÷ 24 semi-monthly, ÷ 26 bi-weekly, ÷ 52 weekly.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Both columns divide by the same 260 weekdays for their daily and hourly rows. That is
      deliberate: the adjusted daily rate answers "what does an average day of the year earn me",
      which only means anything spread across the whole year. Dividing by 235 instead would simply
      hand back the unadjusted rate you started from.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Take <strong>$50 an hour</strong>, 40 hours across 5 days, with 10 holidays and 15 vacation
      days. A day is 8 hours, so <strong>$400</strong>. The year holds 260 weekdays, of which you
      work 235.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unadjusted, that is 400 × 260 = <strong>$104,000</strong> — $8,667 a month, $4,000 bi-weekly,
      $2,000 a week. Adjusted, it is 400 × 235 = <strong>$94,000</strong> — $7,833 a month, $3,615
      bi-weekly, $1,808 a week, and an effective <strong>$45.19</strong> an hour once the 25 unpaid
      days are spread back across the year. The $4.81 gap between $50 and $45.19 is exactly what
      the time off costs you.
    </p>

    <h2>Bi-weekly vs. semi-monthly vs. monthly</h2>
    <p>
      "Twice a month" and "every two weeks" are not the same thing. <strong>Bi-weekly</strong>
      divides by 26, the number of paychecks in a year when you are paid fortnightly.
      <strong>Semi-monthly</strong> (the 1st and 15th, say) pays only 24 times, so each check is
      larger. <strong>Monthly</strong> divides by 12. On the $94,000 adjusted figure above, that is
      $3,615, $3,917 and $7,833 respectively — three different numbers describing one salary.
    </p>

    <h2>Match it to your real schedule</h2>
    <p>
      The result is only as good as its assumptions. Change hours per week for overtime or
      part-time work, days per week for a compressed or six-day week, and the two time-off fields
      to whatever your contract actually gives you. Unpaid leave belongs in the vacation field too
      — as far as the arithmetic is concerned, an unpaid day and an unused vacation day cost the
      same.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every figure here is <strong>gross</strong> pay, before income tax, payroll tax or benefits.
      To estimate what you actually keep, try the <a href="/finance/income-tax-calculator">income
      tax calculator</a>; to add up time actually worked, see the
      <a href="/everyday/hours-calculator">hours calculator</a>. These are gross figures for
      general use, not payroll or tax advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
